@page "/badge"

<CatHead Title="Badges">
    <code>.badge</code> is a neutral pill. The semantic modifiers carry the same meaning as the
    button families; the three categorical hues carry none at all.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Semantic" Title="Semantic">
    <p>Use these when the colour means something. They follow the colour-blind toggle.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A badge is a label, not a target, so its sizes are a <strong>type</strong> scale rather than
        the <a href="/concepts">control heights</a>. It has to sit inside a line of text, a table cell
        and a card title without setting the line height, which is why <code>.badge-lg</code> is 12px
        text and not a 36px pill.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Categorical" Title="Categorical">
    <p>
        For distinguishing categories that have no ranking — a type, a queue, a source. Reach
        for these only after the semantic set, and never to imply good or bad.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Severity" Title="Severity — an ordered scale" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For a scale whose steps <strong>rank</strong>: an incident priority P1–P5, a CVSS band, a
        triage tier. The semantic set ranks nothing, and the categorical hues say out loud that they
        do not — so neither can serve an ordering, which is why this is its own family.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The order is in the fill, not in the hue.</strong> Step 1 is an opaque pill and 2–5
        are tints that weaken as the rank falls. That ladder is what survives the colour-blind
        palette, a greyscale print and forced colours; a hue rotation cannot carry an order, which is
        exactly why hue is identity here and never the ranking. Under
        <code>data-cvd="1"</code> step 1 darkens away from the brand and step 2 leaves the warm band
        for violet, because coral, crimson, orange and amber all converge under deuteranopia.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The label has to say the rank.</strong> “P2”, “High”, “3 of 5” — a bare coloured pill
        is a colour, not a position, and nothing announces it as one. The bars on
        <a href="/progress">Progress</a> take the same ramp through
        <code>.progress-bar--sev-N</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

